/* Generated by sim/gen_peripheral_art.py -- do not hand-edit; rerun the
 * generator and paste its stdout.
 *
 * Source art: 像素画参考/01_end_rabbit_lineart2.png, the hooded rabbit
 * from the "01 EOUS" sticker, redrawn as 2-tone line art.
 *
 * 68x69, stride 9, 621 bytes of bitmap. widgets/screen_peripheral.c blits it
 * at design (0, 55), the slot the eight-frame animated cat overlay used to
 * own, below the status row that ends at y18.
 *
 * Two steps. First the drawing is reproduced on its own terms. Each cell
 * knows what share of it the drawing inks, and compares that with the
 * same share over its 5x5 neighbourhood: inkier than its surroundings
 * by 10% and it is a stroke on the light ground; nearly all ink (95%)
 * and it is the dark ground itself. That keeps whichever value is the
 * minority wherever it sits, which is what the reduction needs here --
 * the drawing outlines itself with 32-43 source pixels, two to three
 * cells, but it draws the jacket with 4-7 pixels of paper left white
 * inside a solid mass, a third of a cell, and a single fixed cut can
 * keep one of the two. So the ears close, the bandana keeps the "01"
 * with no crease through it, and the jacket keeps the seams, the
 * placket, the two eyelets and the cross
 * on the shoulder.
 *
 * Three things are drawn over the cut because a cell cannot hold them:
 * the screws, which the reference gives its rings eight source pixels
 * -- half a cell, so the cut returns a smear where a ring with a slot
 * across it belongs -- and so it is one head stamped twice: a five-cell
 * face cleared inside a seven-cell ring inked between 2.5 and 3.5 cells
 * from the centre, the only band that closes into a circle at this
 * size; the plate of the right ear, which the cut leaves a checkerboard
 * because a nine-cell-wide mass is mostly edge, taken from the
 * reference's own outline with the square corner dropped off its lower
 * left; and the "EOUS", which the reference draws five cells wide and
 * the cut fills solid. The screw and plate geometry is flood-filled off
 * the reference, not read by eye. Both heads carry the same five-cell
 * plus, and the plate carries five paper dots stretched along the band.
 * The word is set from a drawn pixel alphabet, one cell of the grid to
 * one cell of the drawing: 13 columns by 8 rows, its letters stepping
 * up the band at 0.29 rows to the column -- 16 degrees, one row per
 * letter across its 3.5-column step, which is the tilt the word had
 * before, with the U lifted a row further as the only exception. The
 * band's own lower edge is steeper, half a row to the column; that
 * was drawn last round and he has asked the old tilt
 * back. The word is set four columns right of the reference word's
 * centre and three rows above where it stood: at its own centre the
 * first letter fuses with the foot of the bandana's "1", and where it
 * stood on the band's edge the halo that makes room for the letters
 * erased 23 cells of that edge, which is the band losing its own
 * outline. The halo alone takes 7.
 *
 * Last of all comes the hand: 215 cells set to ink and 110 to paper by the
 * owner -- four lists he sent back, folded into one set -- clicking over
 * this art in sim/像素微调.html. It runs after every
 * stamp, so where the hand argues with the cut, the hand wins -- this is
 * where the picture stops being a reduction of the reference and starts
 * being his drawing.
 * The reference gives itself 1.4 cells to a letter there and no typeface
 * reads at that size, so the alphabet is bigger than the original.
 *
 * Four more are taken from the reference at half a cell rather than
 * judged against their neighbourhood: the two eye arcs, which are paper
 * boxed in by ink, so the neighbourhood mean over their window is high and
 * a white cell has to be very white indeed to survive it. Read on their
 * own instead, each arc is one stroke of a constant pen -- 2.0 to 2.6 cells
 * across from end to end -- and the two together are 45 cells of paper,
 * which is what the rectangle is stamped with; the slash inside the
 * counter of the "0", one cell wide and ten long,
 * which the cut breaks in two where the stroke inks only a sixth of
 * the cell; the bandana's left silhouette, a half-covered edge
 * against the white jacket, over whose rectangle alone the plain
 * half-coverage rule is unioned with the cut so the outline arrives
 * in one piece; and the floating heart, the one figure that stands
 * alone on empty paper. There the neighbourhood rule is the wrong
 * instrument -- a cell of it is judged against the figure's own
 * inside or against nothing -- so the rectangle is flood-filled at
 * source resolution, which gives exactly two components, and they
 * are taken one at a time. The outline is then thinned rather than
 * cut: the brush that drew it is two to three cells wide, so any cut
 * keeps a band one cell thick in places and two in others, and a
 * two-cell place reads as a stray pixel welded onto the line. The
 * peeled centre line comes back 60 cells, one wide throughout,
 * closed, with no end cell. The heart inside it cannot be cut
 * either: it is an arrow, because its cleft is a cell and a half
 * deep, and shifting the sampling grid by a quarter or a half cell
 * gives the same solid parallelogram every time. So that one is set
 * from the typeface, eight cells square, cut at half a cell and
 * leaned 16 degrees. Which typeface is the whole of it: Noto Sans SC
 * sets this character nine columns wide with a bottom two rows deep,
 * and leaned that reads as a slab with a bite out of it, which is
 * what shipped until now. Arial sets it eight wide and tapers it to
 * one cell, and the same lean leaves a notch two cells wide between
 * lobes two and three wide. The reference leans its own heart 31 --
 * the angle its blob matches its mirror image at, IoU 0.893 -- but a
 * lean is paid for in cells: on Arial the notch survives 16 and 20
 * degrees, at 24 the near lobe merges into the row under it, and at
 * 31 the figure is a parallelogram again. Eight rows is still two
 * above the source's own 5.7, and that slack is what the lean costs.
 *
 * The bits are the drawing's own ink and the inversion is left to the
 * palette, exactly as screen_art.c does it: his config sets
 * CONFIG_NICE_OLED_WIDGET_INVERTED=y, both assets answer with the same
 * guard, and a set bit lights up white on the black ground. So the 34%
 * of the sheet this file inks is the drawing's own dark paint. */

